<section class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Platinum-group metals · Research note</div>
  <h1>$title</h1>
  <p class="deck">$deck</p>
  <p class="byline">Evidence review · <time datetime="$date">25 November 2026</time> · <a href="PRODUCTION.md">Claim register</a></p>
</div></section>
<article>
  <p>Platinum, palladium and rhodium are mined together, refined together and used mainly in vehicle exhaust catalysts. They are often treated as one material. This note sets out what public sources show for each metal separately: where it is mined, how much comes back from recycling, how much of its demand is automotive, and what the customs records can and cannot show.</p>
  <p><strong>Finding.</strong> In 2025 South Africa supplied $pt_sa_share of primary platinum and $rh_sa_share of primary rhodium, but $pd_sa_share of primary palladium, where Russia led with $pd_ru_share. Vehicles took $pt_auto_share of platinum demand, $pd_auto_share of palladium demand and $rh_auto_share of rhodium demand. All three markets drew down stocks in 2025. A single figure for the group hides these differences. $jm</p>

  <h2>Mine production differs by metal</h2>
  <p>USGS publishes mine production for platinum and palladium by country, and reserves for the group as a whole. South Africa mined $usgs_pt_sa of the world's platinum in 2024 and Russia $usgs_pt_ru. For palladium the order is reversed: Russia $usgs_pd_ru, South Africa $usgs_pd_sa. Zimbabwe, Canada and the United States supplied most of the rest. USGS gives no mine production by country for rhodium. $mcs2026</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Mine production and reserves by country">
    <table>
      <caption>Mine production in tonnes of metal, and reserves of all platinum-group metals. USGS, 2026 edition. The 2025 column is an estimate.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Country</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum 2024</th><th scope="col" class="n">Share</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum 2025</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium 2024</th><th scope="col" class="n">Share</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium 2025</th><th scope="col" class="n">Reserves</th><th scope="col" class="n">Share of listed reserves</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$usgs_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">Shares for 2024 use the published world totals. USGS rounds the 2025 world totals to two figures, so no 2025 shares are shown. The Russian figures are USGS estimates in both years. The world reserve figure is published as “more than 76,000 tonnes”; reserve shares are of the countries listed, which sum to $reserve_sum tonnes. The country concentration index (HHI, zero to 10,000) for 2024 mine output is $hhi_pt for platinum and $hhi_pd for palladium. $mcs2026</p>
  <p>Reserves are an estimate of what can be mined economically under current conditions, and they are revised. Between the 2025 and 2026 editions USGS cut its Russian reserve figure from 16,000 to 11,000 tonnes and its world figure from “more than 81,000” to “more than 76,000” tonnes. It also revised 2024 mine production upward for both leading producers: palladium from an estimated $rev_pd_old to $rev_pd_new tonnes worldwide, and platinum from $rev_pt_old to $rev_pt_new tonnes. $mcs2025 $mcs2026</p>

  <h2>The shares have been stable since 2000</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Mine production shares since 2000">
    <table>
      <caption>Share of world mine production. Each year is taken from the USGS edition published two years later, where it is no longer an estimate.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum · South Africa</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum · Russia</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium · Russia</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium · South Africa</th><th scope="col">USGS edition</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$history_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>South Africa's share of platinum stayed between $pt_sa_min and $pt_sa_max over the period. Russia's share of palladium fell from $pd_ru_first in 2000 to $pd_ru_last in 2024, and South Africa's rose from $pd_sa_first to $pd_sa_last. The two countries together mined $pt_two of platinum and $pd_two of palladium in 2024, so other producers supply about a fifth. $history_sources</p>
  <p><strong>Correction, 25 November 2026.</strong> The previous version of this page showed a chart titled “Platinum mine production” with South Africa at 47% in 2000 and 57% in 2024. That series is the British Geological Survey line for all six platinum-group metals added together, not platinum. It remains a valid measure of the group: $bgs_summary. The text beside it said South Africa held “57–60% throughout”, which did not match its own first value. $bgs</p>

  <h2>Supply and demand in 2025, metal by metal</h2>
  <p>Johnson Matthey publishes a yearly balance for each metal. Its supply figures are producer sales, allocated to the region where the metal was mined, so they differ from USGS mine output. Its recycling figures count only metal that returns to the open market. Metal that an industrial user recycles and keeps is netted out of demand instead. $jm</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Supply and demand by metal in 2025">
    <table>
      <caption>Tonnes of metal, 2025. Johnson Matthey, May 2026. Converted from thousand troy ounces, so a figure can differ by 0.1 from the tonnes printed in the report.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium</th><th scope="col" class="n">Rhodium</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$balance_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>All three markets were in deficit in 2025: demand exceeded supply and stocks fell. The automotive share of demand is the main difference between the metals. Platinum has large jewellery, industrial and investment uses. Palladium and rhodium depend on vehicles for more than four-fifths of demand. $jm</p>

  <h2>What recycling adds, and when</h2>
  <p>Recycling supplied $pt_sec_share of platinum, $pd_sec_share of palladium and $rh_sec_share of rhodium reaching the market in 2025. Most of it is scrap from vehicles: $pt_sec_auto of recycled platinum, $pd_sec_auto of recycled palladium and all of the recycled rhodium that Johnson Matthey reports. $jm</p>
  <p>Metal inside vehicles on the road is not a stock that can be drawn on in a supply interruption. It returns when vehicles are scrapped, which the industry association puts at 15 to 18 years after sale, and only where converters are collected and processed. Johnson Matthey reports that recycling in Europe, North America and Japan was unusually weak in 2023 and 2024 because owners kept vehicles longer. The amount recycled in a year therefore follows past vehicle sales and scrappage, not the current price or a current shortage. $ipa_recycling $jm</p>

  <h2>Two screens of exposure</h2>
  <p><strong class="note">Modelled.</strong> The first screen removes $cut_pct of one country's primary sales for a year and holds everything else at its 2025 value. It gives the lost metal as a share of 2025 demand, before any use of stocks or extra recycling. It carries no probability.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Supply interruption screen">
    <table>
      <caption>Modelled. Metal lost if $cut_pct of a country's 2025 primary sales did not arrive, as a share of 2025 demand.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Interruption</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium</th><th scope="col" class="n">Rhodium</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$cut_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>A South African interruption weighs most on platinum and rhodium. A Russian interruption weighs most on palladium. For comparison, the 2025 deficits were $pt_deficit_share of demand for platinum, $pd_deficit_share for palladium and $rh_deficit_share for rhodium.</p>
  <p><strong class="note">Modelled.</strong> The second screen lowers automotive demand and holds supply and all other demand at their 2025 values. It shows the resulting market balance. Battery-electric vehicles need no exhaust catalyst, so this is the direction of the long-run change, but the size and timing of any fall are not estimated here.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Automotive demand screen">
    <table>
      <caption>Modelled. Market balance in tonnes if automotive demand were lower and nothing else changed. A negative value is a deficit.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Automotive demand</th><th scope="col" class="n">Platinum</th><th scope="col" class="n">Palladium</th><th scope="col" class="n">Rhodium</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
$demand_rows
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>With automotive demand a quarter lower, palladium and rhodium would move into surplus and platinum would stay close to balance. The screen leaves out two effects that work in the other direction. The three metals come out of the same ore, so a mine that cut output because palladium was in surplus would also produce less platinum and rhodium. And vehicle scrap is most of the recycled supply, so fewer combustion vehicles would later mean less recycled metal. Neither effect is measured here. $ipa_rhodium $usgs_pp1802</p>

  <h2>What the customs records cover</h2>
  <p>The trade table uses four customs codes. Three cover unwrought metal or powder, one each for platinum, palladium and rhodium. The fourth, 711510, covers catalysts in the form of wire cloth or grill of any platinum-group metal, which are industrial gauzes. Vehicle catalytic converters are not in any of the four. Before 2022 they were classified with other gas-filtering equipment under 842139, and a separate code, 842132, exists only from the 2022 nomenclature. Catalysts on a support are classified under 3815. The table therefore shows trade in refined metal, much of it passing through refining and trading hubs, and says nothing about where converters are made. $wco_ch71 $wco_2022</p>
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="control" id="static-trade" hidden><label class="note" for="trade-slider">Trade year</label><input id="trade-slider" type="range" min="0" max="0" value="0"><b id="trade-year">$trade_year</b></div>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="BACI trade data">
      <table>
        <caption>World exports by customs code. The latest year in the extract is $trade_year. Values are nominal US dollars.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Traded category</th><th scope="col">World trade</th><th scope="col">Leading exporter</th><th scope="col">Coverage</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody id="trade-body">
$trade_rows
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Tonnage check.</strong> The extract records $anomaly_2002 tonnes of palladium trade in 2002 and $anomaly_2009 tonnes in 2009, against $typical_low to $typical_high tonnes in the other years. Six bilateral records explain the difference: five imports into Canada in 2002 and one shipment from the United Kingdom to Cyprus in 2009. Each carries a weight about a thousand times what its value implies, at around one US cent per gram when palladium traded at several dollars per gram. Without them the totals are $clean_2002 tonnes for 2002 and $clean_2009 tonnes for 2009. This page uses trade values only. The tonnage series should not be used for those two years. $baci</p>

  <h2>What remains open</h2>
  <p>Mine shares, recycling shares and automotive shares are measured. Three questions that matter for supply security are not. The first is how mine economics link the metals: whether lower palladium demand would cut platinum and rhodium output. The second is refining capacity by country, which the public sources do not give. The third is how recycled supply will change as the vehicle fleet changes. Rhodium, iridium and ruthenium have no published mine production by country in the USGS series, so their supply geography rests on producer-sales estimates.</p>

  <h2>Revision note · 25 November 2026</h2>
  <p>The previous overview treated the three metals as one material. It said that two countries were “essentially the only” mine sources, that recycled metal supplied “a quarter or more” of supply and cushioned supply shocks as a hidden reserve, and that the real threat was a collapse in vehicle demand. This revision gives each metal's figures, corrects the mislabelled history chart, replaces the reserve-cushion claim with what the recycling figures measure, and replaces the demand claim with a labelled screen. The <a href="PRODUCTION.md">claim register</a> lists each change.</p>

  <h2>Sources and downloads</h2>
